//! Zamani Quantum Scheduling — Scheduled Operation Model
//!
//! This module defines the scheduler-owned representation of one executable
//! operation.
//!
//! ============================================================================
//! ARCHITECTURAL CONTRACT
//! ============================================================================
//!
//! This file answers:
//!
//!     "What information does the scheduler need in order to schedule one
//!      already-defined quantum/hybrid operation?"
//!
//! It does NOT redefine quantum semantics.
//!
//! The canonical semantic operation remains owned by:
//!
//!     crate::quantum::ir
//!
//! In particular, this file MUST NOT define another:
//!
//! - QubitId;
//! - PhysicalQubitId;
//! - Gate;
//! - QuantumOperation;
//! - QuantumCircuit;
//! - Measurement;
//! - quantum instruction semantic model.
//!
//! The authoritative logical qubit identity is:
//!
//!     crate::quantum::ir::qubit::QubitId
//!
//! Physical placement remains a routing concern.
//!
//! ============================================================================
//! DEPENDENCY DIRECTION
//! ============================================================================
//!
//! ```text
//!                         quantum::ir
//!                             │
//!                             │ semantic operation
//!                             ▼
//!                  scheduling::adapters::ir
//!                             │
//!                             │ validated scheduling view
//!                             ▼
//!              scheduling::ir::operation
//!                             │
//!              ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
//!              ▼              ▼              ▼
//!          dependency      resources       timing
//!              │              │              │
//!              └──────────────┼──────────────┘
//!                             ▼
//!                         scheduler
//! ```
//!
//! Scheduling therefore consumes semantic operations; it does not become the
//! owner of their meaning.
//!
//! ============================================================================
//! WRITE-ONCE / SCALE-EVERYWHERE CONTRACT
//! ============================================================================
//!
//! A scheduling operation contains no machine-size assumptions.
//!
//! It must support:
//!
//! - one qubit;
//! - arbitrarily many qubits;
//! - arbitrary operation arity;
//! - dynamic circuits;
//! - measurements;
//! - reset;
//! - classical dependencies;
//! - communication operations;
//! - logical operations;
//! - routed physical operations;
//! - QEC operations;
//! - pulse-level semantic operations;
//! - analog operations;
//! - future operation kinds.
//!
//! No constant in this file limits:
//!
//! - number of qubits;
//! - number of operands;
//! - number of resources;
//! - number of operations;
//! - circuit depth;
//! - operation duration;
//! - resource capacity.
//!
//! Concrete limits belong to scheduling policy, resource availability,
//! `SchedulingLimits`, the host environment, or the execution target.
//!
//! "Infinity" therefore means:
//!
//!     no artificial finite scheduler ceiling.
//!
//! A concrete compilation remains bounded by available memory, time,
//! address space, target resources, and explicit policy limits.
//!
//! ============================================================================
//! IMMUTABILITY
//! ============================================================================
//!
//! `SchedulingOperation` is immutable after construction.
//!
//! This is deliberate:
//!
//! - planners can share operations safely;
//! - dependency analysis cannot silently change operation semantics;
//! - resource analysis cannot mutate the operation;
//! - deterministic scheduling becomes easier;
//! - parallel schedulers can read operations concurrently;
//! - stale-state bugs are reduced.
//!
//! ============================================================================
//! SAFETY
//! ============================================================================
//!
//! Rust 1.97 / 1.97.1.
//!
//! No unsafe code.
//!
//! No global mutable state.
//!
//! No provider SDKs.
//!
//! No hardware access.
//!
//! No network access.
//!
//! No filesystem access.
//!
//! No scheduling algorithm is implemented here.
//!
//! ============================================================================

